  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q42-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "42",
    "prompt": "Find rational $a$ and $b$ such that\n$$\\frac1{(1+\\sqrt2)^3}=a+b\\sqrt2.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=-7,\\quad b=5$\n\nRationalise $\\frac1{1+\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2-1$, then expand the cube.",
      "worked": "Rationalise the reciprocal first:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{1+\\sqrt2}&=\\frac{\\sqrt2-1}{(1+\\sqrt2)(\\sqrt2-1)}\\\\\\frac1{1+\\sqrt2}&=\\sqrt2-1\\end{align*}$$\nUsing Pascal coefficients $1,3,3,1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{(1+\\sqrt2)^3}&=(\\sqrt2-1)^3\\\\\\frac1{(1+\\sqrt2)^3}&=2\\sqrt2-6+3\\sqrt2-1\\\\\\frac1{(1+\\sqrt2)^3}&=-7+5\\sqrt2\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients gives $a=-7$ and $b=5$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
